            <ArticleTitle>Influence of activity patterns in fitness during youth.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>325-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1055/s-0031-1297955</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The aim of this study was to analyze longitudinal associations between cardiorespiratory fitness, physical activity and body mass index in a 4-year longitudinal study. 170 students (97 girls and 73 boys) aged at baseline from 11 to 17 years were followed. Students performed 20-m-SR; physical activity patterns and parents' education were evaluated using a standard questionnaire. Body mass index was categorized according to established cut points. In a multilevel analysis using MLwIN, 2 level structures were defined: first for individuals and second for time observations. In a longitudinal 2 level analysis, cardiorespiratory fitness was -negatively associated with body mass index for girls and boys, respectively (p&lt;0.05; R2=0.63; 0.62), especially with obesity category (p&lt;0.01; R2=0.58; 0.60). In girls, independent associations were observed between CRF and PA categories regarding participation &quot;almost every day&quot; in organized (p&lt;0.05; R2=0.50) and non-organized sports outside school (p&lt;0.05; R2=0.52) and participation in sports competitions (p&lt;0.05; R2=0.51). In boys, associations were found only with participation in sports competitions (p&lt;0.05; R2=0.50). The results highlight the importance of youth participation in organized activities and competitive sports over time to achieve health-related fitness benefits.</AbstractText>
